      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by finding the original height and radius of the cone. The circumference of the base of the cone is given by \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The formula for the circumference of a circle is \\(2\\pi r\\), so we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[2\\pi r = 16\\pi\\]\n\nBy dividing both sides by \\(2\\pi\\), we get:\n\n\\[r = 8\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the original height of the cone. The slant height \\(l\\) of the cone can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the radius, height, and slant height:\n\n\\[l = \\sqrt{r^2 + h^2} = \\sqrt{8^2 + 30^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 900} = \\sqrt{964} = 2\\sqrt{241}\\]\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\n\\[V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values \\(r = 8\\) and \\(h = 30\\), we get:\n\n\\[V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = 640\\pi\\]\n\nThe volume of the shorter cone is given as \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Let the shorter height be \\(h'\\). Using the volume formula for the shorter cone:\n\n\\[192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h'\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot h' = \\frac{64\\pi}{3} h'\\]\n\nBy dividing both sides by \\(\\frac{64\\pi}{3}\\), we find:\n\n\\[h' = \\frac{192\\pi}{\\frac{64\\pi}{3}} = \\frac{192\\pi \\cdot 3}{64\\pi} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9\\]\n\nThe ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\\frac{h'}{30} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\\]",
