      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the ratio of the shorter height to the original height of the cone. Let's start by finding the original height of the cone.\n\nThe circumference of the base of the cone is given by \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The circumference \\(C\\) of a circle is given by \\(C = 2\\pi r\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius. Therefore, we can find the radius \\(r\\) as follows:\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi \\implies r = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nFor the original cone, the height \\(h\\) is 30 inches. Substituting \\(r = 8\\) and \\(h = 30\\) into the volume formula, we get:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 1920 = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we know that the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Let the height of the shorter cone be \\(h'\\). Using the volume formula for the shorter cone, we have:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r'^2 h'\n\\]\nwhere \\(r'\\) is the radius of the base of the shorter cone. Since the radius \\(r'\\) is proportional to the height \\(h'\\), we can use the ratio of the volumes and the radii to find \\(h'\\). The ratio of the volumes of the two cones is the same as the ratio of the cubes of their heights. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{192\\pi}{640\\pi} = \\frac{h'^3}{30^3} \\implies \\frac{3}{10} = \\frac{h'^3}{2700} \\implies h'^3 = \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 2700 = 810 \\implies h' = \\sqrt[3]{810}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we do not need to find \\(r'\\) explicitly to find the ratio \\(h' / 30\\). We can use the relationship between the volumes directly:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h' \\implies 192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 h' \\implies 192\\pi = \\frac{64\\pi h'}{3} \\implies 576 = 64 h' \\implies h' = \\frac{576}{64} = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, the height of the shorter cone is 9 inches. The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}{30} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\\).",
